From d9140dceed5323e0add964fdfa6a0f7a0aa40abe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noah Evans <noah.evans@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 21:01:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 28/29] hw/ssi: model the Semtech SX1276 LoRa transceiver

The part an RFM9x module is built around, and the radio on a PyCubed: an
SX1276 with its matching network and crystal, no processor and no
firmware of its own.  It is a register file on SPI with a 256-byte FIFO,
and everything a RadioHead-style link layer does -- header, sequence
number, acknowledgement, retry -- runs on the host.

Written from the SX1276 datasheet register map.  Only LoRa mode is
modelled and FSK/OOK is refused with LOG_GUEST_ERROR rather than
half-implemented, because a model that quietly accepted an FSK
configuration would let a driver believe it had configured something.
Three more refusals in the same spirit: changing LongRangeMode outside
sleep, which the datasheet forbids; transmitting with RegPayloadLength
zero; and writing a read-only register.

The air interface is a QEMU NIC, so -netdev socket,mcast= gives a
broadcast medium and two instances in a group hear each other.  Frames
are bare LoRa payloads, not Ethernet.  What it does not model is said in
the file: no range, no collisions, no interference, and by default no
loss -- which is the opposite of the medium an acknowledge-and-retry
layer exists for, so there is a drop-pct property, defaulting to zero,
without which that path in a guest driver is never exercised at all.  It
draws from a seeded GRand so a run with loss is reproducible.
